Distributed web infrastructure (three servers) for www.foobar.com

Flow:
The user's browser resolves www.foobar.com through the DNS; the www A
record points to the load balancer's IP. HAProxy receives the request and
forwards it to one of the two servers. Each server runs Nginx, an
application server, the code base and MySQL. Writes go to the Primary
database on server 1, which replicates them to the Replica on server 2.

Why each new element:
- Load balancer (HAProxy): spreads traffic across the servers and stops
  sending requests to a server that is down.
- Second server: shares the load and provides redundancy.
- MySQL Replica: offloads read queries and keeps a copy of the data.

Load balancer algorithm:
Round Robin - requests are sent to each server in turn (1, 2, 1, 2...),
so the load is shared evenly.

Active-Active vs Active-Passive:
This setup is Active-Active: both servers serve traffic at the same time.
In Active-Passive, only one node serves traffic and the other stays on
standby, taking over only if the active one fails.

Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) cluster:
The Primary records every change in its binary log. The Replica reads
that log and applies the same changes, keeping a near real-time copy.

Primary vs Replica for the application:
The application sends all writes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) to the Primary.
Reads can go to either node. The Replica is read-only.

Issues:
- SPOF: the single load balancer and the single Primary database. If the
  load balancer fails the site is unreachable; if the Primary fails no
  writes are possible.
- Security issues: no firewall, so every port is exposed; no HTTPS, so
  traffic travels in clear text and can be read or modified.
- No monitoring: no way to know when a server is down, overloaded or out
  of disk space until users notice.